</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) aligns large language models by optimizing pairwise preferences and has shown remarkable effectiveness as a simple and scalable alternative to RLHF. However, in practice, preference data are often noisy. Real-world datasets contain both hard noise, where preference labels are reversed, and ambiguous comparisons, where the preferred and rejected responses are nearly indistinguishable. Such noise introduces significant robustness challenges for DPO-based training. Existing robust variants of DPO mainly rely on uniform objective modifications or global reweighting. While partially effective, these methods treat noisy samples as a homogeneous source of uncertainty and fail to distinguish between different noise types, leading to sub-optimal alignment robustness.
In this work, we show that robust preference alignment benefits from addressing different noise types with targeted interventions rather than uniform regularization. We propose <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">w</span>insorized <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">D</span>irect <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P</span>reference <span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">O</span>ptimization (wDPO), a robust LLM alignment approach with hierarchical winsorization. Specifically, wDPO adopts a reward-free hierarchical intervention strategy that leverages only signals already available during DPO training. It first uses the implicit margin from DPO log-ratio to identify heterogeneous noise patterns without relying on external reward models. For hard noise, wDPO performs a data-level intervention by sparsely correcting strongly inconsistent preference pairs. For ambiguous comparisons, it applies a gradient-level intervention through soft winsorization, capping extreme losses in the high-loss tail to prevent weakly informative samples from dominating gradient updates. Extensive experiments on PKU-SafeRLHF and multiple external safety benchmarks demonstrate that wDPO consistently improves preference alignment quality and robustness over vanilla DPO and strong DPO-family baselines, with particularly pronounced gains under controlled label-flip noise. Code is available at <a href="https://anonymous.4open.science/r/wDPO-7BBC" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://anonymous.4open.science/r/wDPO-7BBC</a>.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>LLM Alignment, Robust Preference Optimization, Hierarchical Winsorization
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Aligning large language models (LLMs) with human preferences is essential for developing capable and safe AI systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Achiam et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
While Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Christiano et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2022a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2022b</a>)</cite> established the dominant paradigm, its training complexity and sensitivity to reward model quality have motivated simpler alternatives.
In practice, RLHF pipelines often rely on policy-gradient methods such as PPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulman et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, which can be unstable and require careful hyperparameter tuning.
Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al., 2023</a>)</cite> has therefore emerged as a standard approach, offering an efficient and scalable way to align LLMs by directly optimizing a preference objective over pairwise comparison data.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.07211v1/motivation_.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/123;" width="476" height="123" alt="xxx">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1. </span> Left: Reward-score gaps in a preference dataset. Reversed pairs exhibit negative gaps, while a large fraction of samples cluster near zero, corresponding to ambiguous comparisons.
Right: Under standard DPO, all preference pairs are treated uniformly, causing a small subset of hard or ambiguous pairs to dominate the batch loss and gradient energy, which slows and destabilizes preference separation.
wDPO introduces a hierarchical intervention: it sparsely corrects strongly inconsistent pairs and applies soft winsorization to cap the high-loss tail, thereby achieving stable and higher margin compared with DPO.<span id="acmlabel1" class="ltx_acm_description ltx_centering ltx_nodisplay">xxx</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Despite its success, DPO depends critically on the quality of preference supervision, which in real-world datasets is often imperfect <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2022a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ganguli et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. As a result, a growing body of work has explored robust preference alignment under noisy supervision. In general, current robust optimization methods mainly rely on uniform regularization of the preference objective, implicitly treating noise as a single, homogeneous source of uncertainty. Under this perspective, robustness is enforced by globally softening preference margins, reweighting losses, or guarding against worst-case distributions. Representative examples include objective-level modifications such as rDPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chowdhury et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and cDPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mitchell, 2023</a>)</cite>, distributionally robust formulations such as Dr.DPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and approaches that rely on external reward models or classifiers to rescore and reconstruct preference data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. While effective in certain regimes, these methods apply the same form of correction across all preference pairs and therefore fail to account for the heterogeneity of noise in real-world data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Looking beyond such uniform robustness objectives, a closer examination of DPO training reveals a more nuanced picture. We show that robustness challenges in DPO stem from the <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">heterogeneous nature of preference noise</em>, where different noise types influence optimization dynamics in fundamentally different ways. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#acmlabel1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ wDPO: Winsorized Direct Preference Optimization for Robust LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (left), an analysis of a typical safety preference dataset reveals two prevalent forms of noise. <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hard noise</span> arises when preference labels are reversed, contradicting the underlying reward signal. In contrast, <span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ambiguous comparisons</span> occur when the preferred and rejected responses are nearly indistinguishable, forming a dense cluster near the decision boundary.
Although ambiguous comparisons are not incorrect, they often induce large gradients with limited learning value.
Crucially, these two forms of noise manifest their impact through a shared optimization pathology. As shown in Figure <a href="#acmlabel1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ wDPO: Winsorized Direct Preference Optimization for Robust LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (right), the loss distribution under standard DPO is heavily skewed, with a small fraction of samples accounting for a disproportionate share of the batch loss and gradient energy. Consistent with observations in noisy optimization more broadly <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Finzi et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, we find that these dominant samples largely stem from reversed labels and ambiguous comparisons. They generate large but weakly informative gradients that can destabilize training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
From this perspective, mitigating the undue influence of such high-loss, low-yield samples emerges as a concrete objective for robust preference alignment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Motivated by the above analysis, we propose <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">w</span>insorized <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">D</span>irect <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P</span>reference <span id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">O</span>ptimization (wDPO), a robust approach for LLM alignment under heterogeneous preference noise. The central idea behind wDPO is to move beyond uniform regularization and instead intervene <em id="S1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">hierarchically</em> in the optimization process, reflecting the distinct roles that different noise types play in DPO training. wDPO is designed to operate entirely within the standard DPO framework and leverages only signals that are already available during training. In particular, it uses the implicit DPO reward margin as a consistency signal to characterize heterogeneous noise, without relying on external reward models or additional supervision.
This signal enables wDPO to distinguish between failure modes that require fundamentally different treatments. Strongly inconsistent preference pairs, which are more plausibly attributed to annotation errors, call for <em id="S1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">data-level intervention</em>. In contrast, ambiguous comparisons, which are inherently uncertain but not incorrect, primarily affect training through extreme losses and therefore require <em id="S1.p4.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">gradient-level intervention</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Based on this distinction, wDPO applies two complementary interventions at different stages of optimization. For hard noise, it performs a sparse data-level correction of strongly inconsistent preference pairs, mitigating systematically misleading gradients. For ambiguous comparisons, it applies soft winsorization to the high-loss tail, constraining the influence of extreme but weakly informative losses on gradient updates. By intervening at the appropriate stage for each noise type, wDPO directly addresses the dominant sources of instability identified in the preceding analysis, while preserving all available preference data. We empirically evaluate wDPO on PKU-SafeRLHF and multiple external safety benchmarks. Across a range of settings with both natural and synthetic noise, wDPO consistently improves preference alignment quality and robustness over vanilla DPO and strong DPO-family baselines, with particularly pronounced gains under controlled label-flip noise. Our main contributions are summarized as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:2.0em;">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide an empirical analysis of DPO training under noisy preferences, showing that a small subset of hard noise and ambiguous pairs can dominate gradient updates and destabilize optimization.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose wDPO, a robust LLM alignment approach with <em id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"> hierarchical winsorization</em>. wDPO elaborates a hierarchical intervention strategy:
data-level intervention for hard noise and gradient-level intervention for ambiguous pairs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct extensive experiments on preference alignment benchmarks with both natural and synthetic noise, demonstrating that wDPO consistently improves alignment quality and robustness over DPO-family baselines.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.07211v1/tail_analysis_.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:457/179;" width="457" height="179" alt="xxx">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2. </span>Training dynamics of DPO and wDPO  on Pythia-2.8B. Top row (a–c): DPO trained for 3 epochs. Bottom row (d–f): wDPO  trained for 1 epoch. (a,d) Top Group energy share, measured as the gradient-energy fraction contributed by a fixed top group of samples. (b,e) Global energy uniformity measured by the Herfindahl–Hirschman Index (HHI). (c,f) Preference separation during training, shown by the DPO margin and preference accuracy.<span id="acmlabel2" class="ltx_acm_description ltx_centering ltx_nodisplay">xxx</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Preference-based LLM Alignment</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLM alignment is commonly formulated as a preference-based learning problem, where supervision is provided through relative judgments rather than explicit target outputs.
A canonical instantiation of this paradigm is Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>), which aligns large language models using human preference data.
Given a prompt <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, preferences are provided as pairwise comparisons <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y_{w},y_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y_{w},y_{l})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> is preferred over <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>.
RLHF first trains a reward model <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\phi}(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\phi}(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> from preference data, typically using a pairwise logistic loss:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{RM}}(\phi)=-\mathbb{E}_{(x,y_{w},y_{l})}\left[\log\sigma\big(r_{\phi}(x,y_{w})-r_{\phi}(x,y_{l})\big)\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>RM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{RM}}(\phi)=-\mathbb{E}_{(x,y_{w},y_{l})}\left[\log\sigma\big(r_{\phi}(x,y_{w})-r_{\phi}(x,y_{l})\big)\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the sigmoid function.
The policy <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(y|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(y|x)</annotation></semantics></math> is then optimized to maximize the learned reward while remaining close to a reference policy <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>, via a KL-regularized objective:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\max_{\pi_{\theta}}\;\mathbb{E}_{x,\,y\sim\pi_{\theta}}\big[r_{\phi}(x,y)\big]-\beta\,\mathbb{E}_{x}\Big[\mathrm{KL}\big(\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|x)\,\|\,\pi_{\text{ref}}(\cdot|x)\big)\Big]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></munder><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mi>KL</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{\pi_{\theta}}\;\mathbb{E}_{x,\,y\sim\pi_{\theta}}\big[r_{\phi}(x,y)\big]-\beta\,\mathbb{E}_{x}\Big[\mathrm{KL}\big(\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|x)\,\|\,\pi_{\text{ref}}(\cdot|x)\big)\Big],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> controls the regularization strength.
While effective, RLHF introduces additional complexity due to reward modeling and reinforcement learning, motivating simpler preference-based alternatives.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Direct Preference Optimization</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Direct Preference Optimization (DPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al., 2023</a>)</cite>) is a simplified alternative to RLHF that directly optimizes the policy using preference data, without training an explicit reward model or performing reinforcement learning. Starting from the KL-regularized RLHF objective in Eq. (<a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 2.1. Preference-based LLM Alignment ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ wDPO: Winsorized Direct Preference Optimization for Robust LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), DPO eliminates the reward model by expressing preference learning directly in terms of policy likelihood ratios. Given a preference pair <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y_{w},y_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y_{w},y_{l})</annotation></semantics></math>, DPO defines a log-probability margin:</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E3X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s(x,y_{w},y_{l})=\beta\Bigg[\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y_{w}\mid x)}{\pi_{\theta}(y_{l}\mid x)}-\log\frac{\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{w}\mid x)}{\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{l}\mid x)}\Bigg]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s(x,y_{w},y_{l})=\beta\Bigg[\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y_{w}\mid x)}{\pi_{\theta}(y_{l}\mid x)}-\log\frac{\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{w}\mid x)}{\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{l}\mid x)}\Bigg],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a fixed reference policy and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is a temperature parameter. The DPO objective minimizes a logistic loss over this margin:</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}(\theta)=-\mathbb{E}_{(x,y_{w},y_{l})}\left[\log\sigma\big(s(x,y_{w},y_{l})\big)\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>DPO</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}(\theta)=-\mathbb{E}_{(x,y_{w},y_{l})}\left[\log\sigma\big(s(x,y_{w},y_{l})\big)\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">By directly optimizing the policy with a supervised objective, DPO avoids the instability and complexity associated with reward modeling and reinforcement learning, while retaining strong empirical performance. However, since each preference pair contributes directly to the optimization signal, the training dynamics of DPO are inherently sensitive to the quality and consistency of the underlying preference data. Next, we analyze how heterogeneous preference quality manifests in DPO training and propose a reward-free intervention strategy to control its influence on optimization.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>The Proposed Winsorized DPO</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present wDPO, a robust preference optimization method based on hierarchical intervention over DPO training dynamics.
Rather than applying a uniform correction across all preference pairs, wDPO intervenes at multiple levels using only batch-level optimization signals. Specifically, wDPO combines a data-level label correction and a gradient-level soft intervention to address heterogeneous preference noise.
At the data level, wDPO performs a soft form of label correction that mitigates the impact of likely reversed preference annotations.
This is implemented via a sparse, flip-aware loss mixing mechanism, which selectively interpolates between the original and reversed preference losses for a small set of strongly inconsistent pairs. At the gradient level, wDPO applies soft winsorization to the high-loss tail, capping extreme gradients to limit the influence of ambiguous comparisons. In the following, we first provide evidence of a key failure mode in standard DPO, namely gradient dominance by high-loss pairs, and show how this failure mode motivates the hierarchical intervention adopted by wDPO. Then we present the two intervention stages in detail.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Gradient Dominance as a Failure Mode</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#acmlabel2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ wDPO: Winsorized Direct Preference Optimization for Robust LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> presents a diagnostic analysis of DPO training dynamics under noisy preference supervision. Rather than evaluating final performance, this analysis aims to reveal a failure mode in how gradient contributions are distributed within a batch, which directly motivates our hierarchical intervention design. To characterize this behavior, we track three batch-level signals during training: (i) the tail energy share, defined as the fraction of total gradient energy contributed by the Top-group (report Top <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30\%</annotation></semantics></math>) samples in each batch; (ii) the Herfindahl–Hirschman Index (HHI) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rhoades, 1993</a>)</cite>, which measures the concentration of gradient energy across samples; and (iii) validation margin and preference accuracy as indicators of optimization stability. These signals are used to contrast the training dynamics of standard DPO and wDPO under the same setup (formal definitions are provided in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Tail Energy Share and HHI Metrics ‣ wDPO: Winsorized Direct Preference Optimization for Robust LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figures <a href="#acmlabel2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ wDPO: Winsorized Direct Preference Optimization for Robust LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(a–c) illustrate the evolution of these signals for standard DPO. Early in training, the tail energy share decreases, indicating a relatively diffuse gradient distribution.
However, in the presence of noisy preferences, this behavior does not reflect improved optimization. At this stage, the model has not yet established a reliable preference direction, and both consistent and inconsistent pairs contribute comparably to the update. As training progresses, the model fits a large fraction of consistent pairs, while a small subset of inconsistent or ambiguous pairs remains difficult.
These pairs retain negative or near-zero margins, which induces disproportionately large losses. As a result, their gradient contributions increasingly dominate the batch update. This transition is reflected by the rising tail energy share and the increasing HHI, where fewer than 30% of samples account for more than half of the total gradient energy. Such dominance concentrates optimization on a small number of problematic pairs and destabilizes training, as also reflected by stagnating or degrading validation signals.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Figures <a href="#acmlabel2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ wDPO: Winsorized Direct Preference Optimization for Robust LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(d–f) show how this behavior changes under wDPO. The overall trend of increasing tail energy share remains, but the early diffuse phase observed in DPO is absent. This reflects the effect of gradient-level intervention, which remains active throughout training and limits the influence of extreme losses from the outset.
Figure <a href="#acmlabel2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ wDPO: Winsorized Direct Preference Optimization for Robust LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(d) further disentangles the two intervention stages. The data-level intervention reduces the contribution of strongly inconsistent pairs by softly correcting their effective labels, while the gradient-level intervention caps the magnitude of extreme losses relative to a batch-dependent threshold. Together, these interventions prevent any single subset of pairs from dominating the update, leading to consistently lower concentration as measured by HHI and more stable optimization behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, this analysis demonstrates that standard DPO is vulnerable to gradient dominance arising from inverted and ambiguous preference pairs.
By explicitly controlling gradient contributions at both the data and gradient levels, wDPO addresses this failure mode at its source, which guides the design of the two-stage interventions described next.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Stage I: Margin-aware Soft Label Correction</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Preference supervision is heterogeneous: while most preference pairs are consistent with the emerging preference direction, a small subset can be strongly inverted under the current model. Such inverted preference pairs constitute hard noise, as they induce gradients that point against the dominant update and can disproportionately destabilize DPO training.
Stage I addresses this failure mode with a data-level intervention that performs a sparse and bounded soft correction within each batch.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each preference pair <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{i},y_{w,i},y_{l,i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{i},y_{w,i},y_{l,i})</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}=s(x_{i},y_{w,i},y_{l,i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}=s(x_{i},y_{w,i},y_{l,i})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the DPO margin. We consider two possible preference directions: the observed direction <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(y_{w,i},y_{l,i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(y_{w,i},y_{l,i})</annotation></semantics></math>, and the swapped direction <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(y_{l,i},y_{w,i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(y_{l,i},y_{w,i})</annotation></semantics></math>.
This yields two logistic losses:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{\rightarrow}(x_{i})=-\log\sigma(s_{i}),\qquad\ell_{\leftarrow}(x_{i})=-\log\sigma(-s_{i})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{\rightarrow}(x_{i})=-\log\sigma(s_{i}),\qquad\ell_{\leftarrow}(x_{i})=-\log\sigma(-s_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">If a pair is inverted under the current model, assigning weight to the swapped direction would reduce the loss and better align the update with the prevailing preference signal. Stage I introduces a correction weight <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> for each sample and forms a mixed loss:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\ell}_{i}=(1-w_{i})\,\ell_{\rightarrow}(x_{i})+w_{i}\,\ell_{\leftarrow}(x_{i})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\ell}_{i}=(1-w_{i})\,\ell_{\rightarrow}(x_{i})+w_{i}\,\ell_{\leftarrow}(x_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Most samples retain <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, while only a small number of highly inconsistent pairs receive partial correction. To keep this intervention conservative, we impose a batch-level budget controlled by a single hyperparameter <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>, requiring:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{B}\sum_{i=1}^{B}w_{i}\leq\rho_{f}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>B</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></munderover><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{B}\sum_{i=1}^{B}w_{i}\leq\rho_{f}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.4" class="ltx_p">The correction weights are computed from batch-level signals.
Using the two-direction losses in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 3.2. Stage I: Margin-aware Soft Label Correction ‣ 3. The Proposed Winsorized DPO ‣ wDPO: Winsorized Direct Preference Optimization for Robust LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), we measure the gain from swapping direction as</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}=\ell_{\rightarrow}(x_{i})-\ell_{\leftarrow}(x_{i})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}=\ell_{\rightarrow}(x_{i})-\ell_{\leftarrow}(x_{i}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.5" class="ltx_p">and retain only beneficial gains <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}=\mathrm{ReLU}(g_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ReLU</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}=\mathrm{ReLU}(g_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.
We apply sparsemax <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Martins and Astudillo, 2016</a>)</cite> to the batch score vector <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}=(u_{1},\ldots,u_{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}=(u_{1},\ldots,u_{B})</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain sparse allocation weights <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i}a_{i}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i}a_{i}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, so that only a few pairs with large gains are selected.
These allocations are scaled by the budget as <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{w}_{i}=\rho_{f}B\,a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{w}_{i}=\rho_{f}B\,a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and projected onto <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> while preserving the total budget, which yields the final correction weights <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{w_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{w_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Since margin estimates are unreliable early in training, Stage I is activated only after a warm-up period.
Let <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> be the current training step, <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> the total number of steps, and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> a fixed warm-up ratio.
If <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&lt;\alpha T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&lt;\alpha T</annotation></semantics></math>, we set <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all samples; otherwise the correction weights are computed as above.
In our experiments, we use <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>Stage II: Gradient-oriented Winsorization</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While Stage I mitigates the impact of inverted preference pairs, ambiguous comparisons can still induce persistently large losses.
Such samples tend to form the high-loss tail of a batch and can dominate gradient updates, leading to unstable optimization.
Stage II addresses this issue with a gradient-level intervention that softly caps only the loss tail, limiting the influence of extreme losses while preserving informative gradients.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Stage II operates on the per-sample mixed losses <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\tilde{\ell}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\tilde{\ell}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{B}</annotation></semantics></math> produced by Stage I.
We first define a batch-dependent threshold <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> that marks the onset of the high-loss tail, set as the <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>-quantile of the batch losses:</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(9)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=\mathrm{Quantile}\big(\{\tilde{\ell}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{B},\,q\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Quantile</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=\mathrm{Quantile}\big(\{\tilde{\ell}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{B},\,q\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Samples with <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\ell}_{i}&gt;\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\ell}_{i}&gt;\tau</annotation></semantics></math> are treated as tail samples. The strength of loss capping is adapted to the current batch based on margin consistency. Intuitively, batches with many negative or weakly separated margins are more ambiguous and require stronger regularization.
We therefore compute a batch-level capping budget <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> from the margin statistics and treat it as a constant during backpropagation.
This budget controls the total amount of capping applied within the batch.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Within the tail, capping strength is allocated proportionally to how much each sample exceeds the threshold. Formally, tail losses are softly pulled toward <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> according to per-sample cap weights <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> that satisfy a batch-level budget constraint. The resulting winsorized loss takes the form:</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{\mathrm{win}}_{i}=(1-\lambda_{i})\,\tilde{\ell}_{i}+\lambda_{i}\,\tau." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>win</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{\mathrm{win}}_{i}=(1-\lambda_{i})\,\tilde{\ell}_{i}+\lambda_{i}\,\tau.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Finally, we optimize the average winsorized loss over the batch:</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(11)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\frac{1}{B}\sum_{i=1}^{B}\ell^{\mathrm{win}}_{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>B</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>win</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\frac{1}{B}\sum_{i=1}^{B}\ell^{\mathrm{win}}_{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">The full specification of how <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are computed in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Stage II Implementation Details ‣ wDPO: Winsorized Direct Preference Optimization for Robust LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>. The overall implementation is summarized in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Appendix A Algorithm ‣ wDPO: Winsorized Direct Preference Optimization for Robust LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> in the appendix.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4. </span>Discussion of wDPO</h3>

<section id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Computational Complexity.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">wDPO introduces two batch-level interventions on top of standard DPO training. Both stages operate entirely within each batch and do not require additional models, data preprocessing, or extra forward passes.
Stage I computes per-sample margins and two-direction losses, followed by sparsemax over a batch-sized vector, which incurs <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(B\log B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(B\log B)</annotation></semantics></math> time in practice. Stage II involves quantile computation and linear allocation over the loss tail, which can be implemented in <math id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(B\log B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(B\log B)</annotation></semantics></math> time.
Overall, the additional overhead of wDPO is modest compared to the cost of model forward and backward passes, and does not change the asymptotic training complexity of DPO.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Generality and Applicability.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although wDPO is developed in the context of DPO, its design principles are not tied to a specific preference optimization objective.
Both interventions rely only on batch-level signals that are commonly available in preference-based learning, such as per-sample losses and margin statistics. This makes the proposed hierarchical intervention compatible, in principle, with other preference optimization frameworks that exhibit similar gradient dominance behavior, including variants of DPO with alternative loss formulations. More broadly, our analysis suggests that explicitly controlling gradient influence at multiple levels can be an effective design strategy for stabilizing preference learning under heterogeneous supervision.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide more empirical evidences to show the effectiveness of our proposed wDPO and specific design choices underlying its data-level and gradient-level interventions. Specifically, we investigate the following research questions:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:2.0em;">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1</span>: Does wDPO consistently outperform DPO-family baselines under standard training conditions?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ2</span>: How robust is wDPO when preference supervision is corrupted by injected label-flip noise?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ3</span>: How sensitive is wDPO to its key hyperparameters that control the two intervention stages?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ4</span>: What is the contribution of each component in wDPO , and how do the data-level and gradient-level interventions interact?</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Experimental Settings</h3>

<section id="S4.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.1. </span>Dataset</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following prior safety alignment work, we run experiments on PKU-SafeRLHF-30K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al., 2023</a>)</cite> from PKU-Alignment.
It contains about 29.9K paired comparisons, with 26.9K for training and 3.0K for testing.
Each example consists of a prompt and two responses.
The dataset provides preference labels for helpfulness and safety, and also includes a binary safety tag for each response.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.2. </span>Baselines</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate wDPO against a set of preference alignment baselines.
We include the Vanilla pretrained backbone, SFT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, and DPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
We also include robust DPO variants that target noisy comparisons or distributional uncertainty, including IPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gheshlaghi Azar et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, cDPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mitchell, 2023</a>)</cite>, rDPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chowdhury et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and Dr.DPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.3. </span>Backbones</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We study wDPO on four base LLMs that have not been safety-aligned via RLHF: Pythia-2.8B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Biderman et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, Llama-3.2-3B, Llama-3-8B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">AI@Meta, 2024</a>)</cite>, and Qwen2.5-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team, 2024</a>)</cite>.
These backbones span multiple model families and scales.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.4. </span>Benchmarks and Evaluation Metrics</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate on PKU-SafeRLHF-30K (test split), Do-Not-Answer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, HarmBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mazeika et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, HH-RLHF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2022a</a>)</cite>, and Salad Bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
On the external safety benchmarks, we report Attack Success Rate (ASR; lower is better) as the primary metric.
We compute ASR using two widely adopted safety judges: MD-Judge (MD) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and Nemotron Safety Guard (NV) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Joshi et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
In addition, on PKU-SafeRLHF we report Win Rate (WR; higher is better) based on pairwise judgments from the GPT-4.1 mini API. Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Detail of Experimental Settings ‣ wDPO: Winsorized Direct Preference Optimization for Robust LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> provides full details on baseline configurations, backbones, benchmark setup, and evaluation protocols.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
