<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Main Results (RQ1)</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate wDPO under both in-distribution (IID) and out-of-distribution (OOD) settings to assess whether hierarchical intervention leads to consistently improved safety alignment. Across all settings, wDPO achieves the strongest performance among DPO-family methods, indicating that directly controlling heterogeneous gradient influence is more effective than applying uniform robustness penalties.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">In-Distribution Performance.</span>
Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.1.4. Benchmarks and Evaluation Metrics ‣ 4.1. Experimental Settings ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ wDPO: Winsorized Direct Preference Optimization for Robust LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> reports results on the PKU-SafeRLHF-30K test split. Across all four backbones, wDPO achieves the best overall safety alignment performance. While robust baselines such as cDPO and rDPO improve upon vanilla DPO in several cases, their gains are inconsistent across models and metrics. cDPO adopts a conservative target to reduce overconfidence under noisy supervision, and rDPO assumes a fixed flip rate to debias the loss. Dr.DPO further introduces distributionally robust optimization. However, these methods apply uniform corrections across samples, which limits their ability to address heterogeneous failure sources. In contrast, wDPO explicitly targets two dominant sources of training instability. It applies a sparse, flip-aware correction to mitigate the impact of likely reversed preference pairs, and it caps the high-loss tail to limit the influence of ambiguous comparisons. This targeted design allows wDPO to outperform even strong robust baselines such as Dr.DPO, demonstrating the advantage of sample-adaptive, batch-level intervention over global regularization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Out-of-Distribution Generalization.</span>
Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.1.4. Benchmarks and Evaluation Metrics ‣ 4.1. Experimental Settings ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ wDPO: Winsorized Direct Preference Optimization for Robust LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> summarizes results on external safety benchmarks, including Do-Not-Answer, HarmBench, HH-RLHF, and Salad Bench.
Across all benchmarks, judges, and model backbones, models trained with wDPO consistently outperform DPO-family baselines. Importantly, these gains persist under distribution shifts, where preference patterns and safety criteria differ substantially from those seen during training. This behavior suggests that wDPO does not simply fit the training distribution, but instead improves the underlying optimization dynamics of preference learning. By explicitly limiting the influence of wrong-direction supervision and ambiguous high-loss pairs, wDPO encourages the model to rely on more stable and reliable preference signals during training. As a result, the learned alignment behavior transfers more robustly to unseen safety scenarios, leading to improved generalization beyond the training data.</p>
</div>
