<span id="S4.T3.2.8.13.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T3.2.8.13.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4.14%</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.07211v1/parameters_analysis_.svg" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/127;" width="452" height="127"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3. </span>Hyperparameter sensitivity on Pythia-2.8B, evaluated on PKU-SafeRLHF-30K (test split). Each panel varies one hyperparameter while keeping all others fixed to the default settings in the paper: (a) flip cap <math id="S4.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>, (b) maximum tail cap <math id="S4.F3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{w}^{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>w</mi><mi>max</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{w}^{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>, and (c) tail quantile <math id="S4.F3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Robustness under Label-Flip Noise (RQ2)</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate robustness under explicit annotation noise by conducting controlled label-flip experiments. A fixed fraction of preference labels in the PKU-SafeRLHF-30K training set is randomly flipped, while evaluation is performed on the clean test split. We consider flip rates of 0%, 10%, 20%, and 30%, with results reported in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.2. Main Results (RQ1) ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ wDPO: Winsorized Direct Preference Optimization for Robust LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. As expected, increasing label-flip noise degrades performance for all methods.
However, the degradation patterns differ substantially. Vanilla DPO is the most sensitive because it strictly follows observed preference labels, each flipped annotation consistently drives the update in the wrong direction, leading to rapid performance collapse as noise increases.
Robust variants such as cDPO and rDPO slow down this collapse by introducing global corrections. Nevertheless, their performance still deteriorates markedly at higher flip rates, indicating that uniform robustness penalties are insufficient under heterogeneous, sample-level noise.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Dr.DPO is the strongest baseline under noisy supervision, benefiting from distributionally robust optimization. Even so, wDPO consistently achieves the best performance across all noise levels, including at 30% flips, and this advantage holds across both WR and ASR judges.
This robustness stems from the sample-adaptive nature of wDPO.
Stage I selectively attenuates the influence of pairs that appear reversed under the current margin signal, while Stage II limits the dominance of extreme-loss pairs that become increasingly prevalent as noise grows.
By preventing noisy and ambiguous samples from disproportionately shaping the update, wDPO maintains a more stable training trajectory. Overall, these results show that wDPO degrades more gracefully as label-flip noise increases. Rather than relying on a fixed global correction, hierarchical intervention enables wDPO to adapt to mixed noise patterns within each batch, resulting in substantially improved robustness under severe annotation corruption.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4. </span>Hyper-parameter Analysis (RQ3)</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We analyze the sensitivity of wDPO to its key hyperparameters to assess whether the proposed hierarchical intervention requires careful tuning.
All experiments are conducted on Pythia-2.8B. When varying one hyperparameter, all others are fixed to their default values reported in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS4" title="C.4. Implementation Details ‣ Appendix C Detail of Experimental Settings ‣ wDPO: Winsorized Direct Preference Optimization for Robust LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.4</span></a>. Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.2. Main Results (RQ1) ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ wDPO: Winsorized Direct Preference Optimization for Robust LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> summarizes the results.</p>
<ul id="S4.I2" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:2.0em;">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage I: flip budget <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> The parameter <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> controls the maximum average correction strength applied to inverted preference pairs within a batch. As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.2. Main Results (RQ1) ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ wDPO: Winsorized Direct Preference Optimization for Robust LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, even a small budget (<math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{f}=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{f}=0.05</annotation></semantics></math>) already yields noticeable improvement, which is consistent with the fact that strongly inverted pairs typically constitute only a small fraction of the data. Performance remains stable over a wide range of values from <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.05</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.05</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.4</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating that Stage I is not sensitive to the exact budget as long as correction is applied conservatively. When <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> becomes too large, performance degrades. In this regime, correction begins to affect pairs that are not truly inverted but are merely hard or near-ties, weakening the genuine preference signal. Based on this trade-off, we set <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{f}=0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{f}=0.3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage II: maximum capping strength <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{w}^{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>w</mi><mi>max</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{w}^{\max}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> The parameter <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{w}^{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>w</mi><mi>max</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{w}^{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> determines the upper bound on loss capping strength in Stage II, while the actual capping level is set adaptively per batch. As <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{w}^{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>w</mi><mi>max</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{w}^{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> increases from small values, performance improves and then stabilizes across a broad interval. This behavior aligns with the goal of soft winsorization, which limits the influence of extreme losses while leaving most samples unaffected. When <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{w}^{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>w</mi><mi>max</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{w}^{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> becomes excessively large, performance drops, as overly aggressive capping suppresses useful gradients from genuinely informative hard pairs. We therefore set <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{w}^{\max}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>w</mi><mi>max</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{w}^{\max}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage II: tail quantile <math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> The quantile <math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> determines where the loss tail begins. When <math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is too small, the tail includes many normal samples, and capping suppresses the clean training signal, leading to degraded performance. As <math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> increases, performance improves and remains stable over a moderate range, reflecting effective isolation of clear outliers. When <math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> becomes too large, the tail set becomes too narrow and fails to capture a sufficient number of problematic high-loss pairs, reducing the effectiveness of capping. We set <math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=0.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=0.7</annotation></semantics></math> to balance these effects.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.07211v1/ablation_.svg" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/241;" width="452" height="241" aria-label="xxx"><p>xxx</p></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4. </span>Ablation results with Pythia-2.8B. Bars show WR on the PKU-SafeRLHF-30K test split. The line shows average ASR across five benchmarks, measured by the two judges. We compare standard DPO, wDPO with only Stage I (w/ SI), only Stage II (w/ SII), and the full two-stage wDPO (full).<span id="acmlabel3" class="ltx_acm_description ltx_centering ltx_nodisplay">xxx</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, wDPO exhibits stable performance across broad ranges of its key hyperparameters. The best results consistently arise from moderate intervention strengths at both stages, supporting the design choice of applying conservative, sample-adaptive corrections rather than aggressive global modifications.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5. </span>Ablation Study (RQ4)</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct an ablation study on Pythia-2.8B to examine the individual contributions of the two intervention stages in wDPO. Figure <a href="#acmlabel3" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.4. Hyper-parameter Analysis (RQ3) ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ wDPO: Winsorized Direct Preference Optimization for Robust LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> summarizes the results. Each stage provides clear benefits when applied in isolation. Stage I improves alignment by addressing inverted preference pairs that inject misleading, wrong-direction gradients.
By applying flip-aware loss mixing, Stage I attenuates the influence of these pairs and stabilizes early training. However, the gains from Stage I alone are moderate. This is consistent with our motivation: although inverted pairs can produce large losses, they are typically sparse and occupy only a small portion of the high-loss tail. Stage II yields larger performance improvements on its own. By directly capping the high-loss tail through soft winsorization, Stage II reduces the dominance of extreme-loss samples that frequently arise from hard or ambiguous comparisons. This intervention substantially improves both WR and ASR, indicating that controlling tail-induced gradient dominance is a primary factor in stabilizing preference optimization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Combining the two stages consistently achieves the best performance.
When both stages are enabled, Stage I first reduces the impact of the most strongly inverted pairs, preventing them from acting as extreme outliers.
Stage II then caps the remaining high-loss tail, which is dominated by hard but not necessarily mislabeled pairs. This clear separation of roles makes the two interventions complementary rather than redundant. Together, they implement a hierarchical control over heterogeneous noise sources and lead to the strongest alignment performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Related Works</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Preference Optimization for LLM Alignment.</span>
Aligning LLMs with human preferences is a central challenge.
The dominant early paradigm is RLHF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Christiano et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, which trains a reward model from pairwise comparisons and then optimizes the policy with RL.
Classic RLHF pipelines typically rely on PPO-style policy optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulman et al., 2017</a>)</cite> and careful tuning. They also require sampling during training, which increases cost and variance. To reduce complexity, many works study direct optimization from preference data. DPO is a representative method that turns preference learning into a simple classification-style objective and avoids explicit reward modeling and RL rollouts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. After DPO, several variants adjust the objective or its calibration. Some methods calibrate the implicit reward scale to better reflect preference strength <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Others modify the margin requirement so that only sufficiently strong preferences drive large updates, such as DPO with an offset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Amini et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. There are also reference-free or monolithic formulations that simplify training further, including ORPO and SimPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hong et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meng et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Beyond pairwise contrastive objectives, alternative formulations use different preference signals or decision models, such as RRHF, SLiC-HF, and KTO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ethayarajh et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Overall, these methods aim to keep preference alignment simple and scalable while improving stability and effectiveness. However, these approaches largely assume clean or well-calibrated preference supervision, and do not explicitly address robustness under heterogeneous preference noise. Our work focuses on this setting and studies how to perform reliable preference optimization when supervision is noisy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Robust Alignment under Noisy Preferences.</span>
Several DPO-family methods improve robustness by modifying the training loss.
rDPO explicitly models random label flips and derives a corrected objective, but it requires a noise rate as input <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chowdhury et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Other work introduces a single global control to soften the margin or downweight uncertain pairs, such as cDPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mitchell, 2023</a>)</cite>.
Some methods also adjust the target margin during training to reduce sensitivity to noisy supervision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
A common limitation is that these approaches apply essentially the same rule to all pairs, so they do not distinguish reversed labels from hard but valid comparisons. Another line of work adopts distributionally robust training.
Dr.DPO optimizes a conservative objective over the preference distribution to hedge against heavy-tailed errors and outliers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
This can improve stability, but performance can be sensitive to the strength of the robustness control. A third line relies on external models to denoise the data. For example, Reward-Augmented Data scores responses with a reward model and rebuilds preference pairs using these scores <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
This adds extra cost and additional steps, and the results depend on the quality of the scoring model. In contrast, our method stays within the standard DPO setup and uses only batch-level signals. It keeps all pairs, but it limits which pairs dominate the update. Specifically, it applies a sparse data-level correction to strongly inverted preference pairs and a gradient-level intervention that softly caps the high-loss tail based on batch statistics.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Preference optimization for LLM alignment often assumes reliable supervision.
Under heterogeneous preference noise, however, DPO training exhibits a concrete failure mode: a small subset of problematic pairs can dominate gradient updates, destabilizing optimization and degrading alignment.
To address this issue, we propose wDPO, a robust preference optimization method that intervenes directly in training dynamics while preserving the simplicity of the DPO framework. wDPO introduces a hierarchical, batch-level intervention that separates different noise sources. It applies sparse, margin-aware soft correction to strongly inverted preference pairs and adaptive soft winsorization to the high-loss tail to limit the influence of ambiguous comparisons. Empirically, wDPO consistently outperforms vanilla DPO and strong DPO-family baselines across various settings, with especially pronounced gains under injected label-flip noise—directly reflecting the failure mode identified in our analysis.
More broadly, our results suggest that robustness in preference optimization may require moving beyond uniform objectives toward explicit control of how different samples shape optimization. Understanding and systematically managing the interaction between heterogeneous preference noise and training dynamics remains a fundamental open problem for the community.</p>
</div>
</section>
