<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Additional Experimental Results</h2>

<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1. </span>OOD Results on Smaller Backbones</h3>

<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A3.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ C.4. Implementation Details ‣ Appendix C Detail of Experimental Settings ‣ wDPO: Winsorized Direct Preference Optimization for Robust LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> reports out-of-distribution ASR on four external safety benchmarks for models aligned on PKU-SafeRLHF-30K, using Pythia-2.8B and Llama-3.2-3B backbones.
Across both backbones, wDPO achieves the lowest average ASR and performs best overall.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Relative to the strongest baseline Dr.DPO, the improvements on Pythia-2.8B are larger than those on Llama-3.2-3B.
This pattern is consistent with smaller or earlier-generation backbones being more sensitive to noisy or ambiguous preference pairs during alignment.
By correcting likely flipped pairs and capping the influence of extreme-loss pairs, wDPO yields clearer gains on this setting.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">On Llama-3.2-3B, strong baselines already achieve low ASR, which limits the attainable improvement.
Nevertheless, wDPO remains competitive and often improves upon the best baseline across judges and benchmarks.
Overall, these results suggest that the benefits of wDPO generalize across model families and are not specific to a single backbone.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2. </span>Effect of Flip Warmup Ratio</h3>

<figure id="A4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.07211v1/start_.svg" id="A4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/274;" width="476" height="274" aria-label="xxx"><p>xxx</p></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5. </span>Sensitivity to the flip warmup ratio <math id="A4.F5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> on Pythia-2.8B. WR is measured on the PKU-SafeRLHF test set. ASR is averaged over five benchmarks.<span id="acmlabel4" class="ltx_acm_description ltx_centering ltx_nodisplay">xxx</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We study the sensitivity of the flip warmup ratio <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> in Stage I.
We run wDPO on Pythia-2.8B and vary <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.4</annotation></semantics></math>.
Figure <a href="#acmlabel4" title="Figure 5 ‣ D.2. Effect of Flip Warmup Ratio ‣ Appendix D Additional Experimental Results ‣ wDPO: Winsorized Direct Preference Optimization for Robust LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> reports WR on the PKU test split and the average ASR over five benchmarks.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#acmlabel4" title="Figure 5 ‣ D.2. Effect of Flip Warmup Ratio ‣ Appendix D Additional Experimental Results ‣ wDPO: Winsorized Direct Preference Optimization for Robust LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows that <math id="A4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math> leads to clearly worse performance.
In this setting, flip mixing is enabled from the first update.
However, early margins are often unreliable because the model has not yet formed a stable preference signal.
As a result, many clean pairs can still have negative margins.
Stage I may then assign flip weights to the wrong pairs, introducing incorrect corrections and destabilizing training.
In extreme cases, wDPO can even underperform standard DPO.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">With a small warmup, performance becomes stable.
For <math id="A4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> in the range <math id="A4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.4</annotation></semantics></math>, both WR and ASR remain strong and vary only mildly.
This suggests that Stage I mainly needs to skip the initial transient phase.
After warmup, margins become more informative, and flip mixing is more likely to target truly inconsistent pairs.
If <math id="A4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is too large, Stage I is delayed for too long and its benefit is reduced.
Based on this tradeoff, we set <math id="A4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.3</annotation></semantics></math> in all experiments.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Tail Energy Share and HHI Metrics</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix defines the two training diagnostics used in Figure <a href="#acmlabel2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ wDPO: Winsorized Direct Preference Optimization for Robust LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
Both metrics are computed at each training step from the current batch.</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1. </span>Per-sample gradient energy.</h3>

<div id="A5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a batch of <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> preference pairs.
Let <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the per-sample loss used for backprop on pair <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.
For DPO, <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the standard DPO loss.
For wDPO, <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the winsorized per-sample loss <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{\mathrm{win}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>win</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{\mathrm{win}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}=\nabla_{\theta}\ell_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}=\nabla_{\theta}\ell_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the gradient with respect to model parameters <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>.
The energy of pair <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as the squared gradient norm:</p>
<table id="A5.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(20)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}=\lVert g_{i}\rVert_{2}^{2}=\left\lVert\nabla_{\theta}\,\ell_{i}\right\rVert_{2}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0.1389em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.1389em">=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}=\lVert g_{i}\rVert_{2}^{2}=\left\lVert\nabla_{\theta}\,\ell_{i}\right\rVert_{2}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The set <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{e_{i}\}_{i=1}^{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{e_{i}\}_{i=1}^{B}</annotation></semantics></math> forms the energy distribution within the batch.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.2. </span>Top group energy share.</h3>

<div id="A5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Figure <a href="#acmlabel2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ wDPO: Winsorized Direct Preference Optimization for Robust LLM Alignment" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the top group refers to the top <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-q)</annotation></semantics></math> fraction of pairs ranked by energy, defined for diagnostic purposes.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the energy of pair <math id="A5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> in a batch of size <math id="A5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.
Sort <math id="A5.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{e_{i}\}_{i=1}^{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{e_{i}\}_{i=1}^{B}</annotation></semantics></math> in descending order and take the top <math id="A5.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lceil(1-q)B\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lceil(1-q)B\rceil</annotation></semantics></math> pairs as the tail set <math id="A5.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{tail}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>tail</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{tail}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The tail energy share is</p>
<table id="A5.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{TailShare}=\frac{\sum_{i\in\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{tail}}}e_{i}}{\sum_{i=1}^{B}e_{i}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>TailShare</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>tail</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{TailShare}=\frac{\sum_{i\in\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{tail}}}e_{i}}{\sum_{i=1}^{B}e_{i}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.3. </span>Herfindahl–Hirschman Index.</h3>

<div id="A5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">HHI measures concentration of gradient energy across the batch.
Energies are first normalized into a probability vector:</p>
<table id="A5.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(21)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}=\frac{e_{i}}{\sum_{j=1}^{B}e_{j}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}=\frac{e_{i}}{\sum_{j=1}^{B}e_{j}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The Herfindahl–Hirschman Index is then</p>
<table id="A5.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(22)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{HHI}=\sum_{i=1}^{B}p_{i}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>HHI</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{HHI}=\sum_{i=1}^{B}p_{i}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A5.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">A smaller value indicates that energy is spread across many pairs, while a larger value indicates stronger concentration on a small subset.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</article>
</div>
</div>
